813,3],[8813,6],[8813,3],[8813,1],[9548,0],[9548,2],[8813,4]]","g":1},{"in":["[[-10000,0],[-1098,1],[3470,null]]"],"out":"[[-10000,0],[-1098,1],[3470,null]]","g":1},{"in":["[[-10000,5],[-8266,1],[-5676,2],[1,0],[9602,null],[-10000,5],[10000,null]]"],"out":"[[-10000,5],[-8266,1],[-5676,2],[1,0],[9602,null],[-10000,5],[10000,null]]","g":1},{"in":["[[6451,0]]"],"out":"[[6451,0]]","g":1},{"in":["[[-10000,8],[-10000,0],[-9798,null],[-10000,1],[-9798,4],[-10000,4],[-9798,0],[-9798,8],[-10000,2]]"],"out":"[[-10000,8],[-10000,0],[-9798,null],[-10000,1],[-9798,4],[-10000,4],[-9798,0],[-9798,8],[-10000,2]]","g":1},{"in":["[[2841,0],[2841,null],[10000,4],[10000,4],[10000,1]]"],"out":"[[2841,0],[2841,null],[10000,4],[10000,4],[10000,1]]","g":1},{"in":["[[-4840,1],[-4840,0]]"],"out":"[[-4840,1],[-4840,0]]","g":1},{"in":["[[-10000,3],[-1972,1],[-875,null],[-32,3]]"],"out":"[[-10000,3],[-1972,1],[-875,null],[-32,3]]","g":1}],"lesson":"linked-list-manipulation","statement":"Each node of the list has a `val`, a `next` pointer and a `random` pointer, which points to any node of the list or is `null`.\n\nReturn a **deep copy** of the list: `n` brand-new nodes with the same values, whose `next` and `random` pointers point to the corresponding **new** nodes. No pointer of the copy may point into the original list.\n\nIn the tests, a list is written as `[[val, random_index], …]`, where `random_index` is the position of the node `random` points to, or `null`. The judge prints your copy in the same form and rejects it if it shares any node with the original.","constraints":["The list has between 0 and 1000 nodes","-10⁴ ≤ Node.val ≤ 10⁴","`Node.random` is null or points to a node in the list"],"hints":["Creating the new nodes is easy. The problem is the random pointer: when you copy a node, the node its random points to may not have been copied yet.","Make two passes. First create every copy and remember old → new in a hash map. Then set copy.next = map[old.next] and copy.random = map[old.random].","For O(1) extra space, weave each copy right after its original (A → A' → B → B'). Then A'.random = A.random.next, and finally unweave the two lists."],"explain":"The copy has the same five values, and each random pointer goes to the copy at the same position (13 → index 0, 11 → index 4, and so on), so it prints exactly like the input: `[[7,null],[13,0],[11,4],[10,2],[1,0]]`."}
